Project I: My little malloc()
Authors: 
    Radha Ghate (rsg169) 
    Zeal Shah (zss37)

------------------------------------------------------------

Design Notes:
- Our implementation uses 8-byte headers where the payload size is stored in the upper bits and the allocation flag is in the LSB. All allocations are 8-byte aligned with a minimum chunk size of 16 bytes (8 header + 8 payload).
- We used first-fit to find free chunks, walking the heap from the start and taking the first chunk that fits. Requests are rounded up to multiples of 8, and chunks are split when the leftover space is at least 16 bytes.
- For coalescing, we merge adjacent free chunks on every free() call. If malloc() can't find space after the initial scan, it runs a full coalesce pass and tries once more before returning NULL.
- Error detection validates all pointers against heap bounds, 8-byte alignment, chunk boundary (via heap walk), and allocation status. Double-frees and invalid pointers all print to stderr and exit with code 2.
- Leak detection runs at program exit using atexit(). It scans the heap and reports total leaked bytes and object count.

------------------------------------------------------------

Test Plan:

We broke our tests into a few categories to make sure we were covering everything. The provided tests (memtest, memgrind) handle the core stress and timing cases, so our custom tests focus on the stuff we were more worried about: making sure free() actually releases memory for reuse, that coalescing works regardless of free order, and that bad pointers are always caught. We also made sure to test edge cases like oversized requests and non-aligned sizes since those are easy to break silently. Between all of them we feel confident we're covering normal usage, boundary conditions, error handling, and memory reuse.

memtest.c (provided)
- Allocates 64 objects filling the 4096-byte heap, writes distinct byte patterns, and checks for neighbor corruption
- Compile with -DLEAK to skip frees and trigger the leak detector

memgrind.c (stress testing)
- 5 workloads run 50 times each, printing average time per run
- Task 1: malloc/free a 1-byte object 120 times
- Task 2: malloc 120 1-byte objects then free all
- Task 3: random malloc/free mix until 120 total allocations
- Task 4: 30 objects of varying sizes freed in reverse order
- Task 5: queue simulation with 60 slots, repeatedly dequeue front and enqueue back

tests/test1.c (allocation and data integrity)
- Tests sizes 8, 16, 32, 64 bytes with alignment checks and byte pattern verification
- Also covers alloc-after-free, malloc(0) returning NULL, and free(NULL) as no-op

tests/test2.c (free releases memory for reuse)
- Fills heap with 128 objects, confirms next malloc fails, frees everything, then fills again
- Checks data integrity of the second fill

tests/test3.c (coalescing)
- Frees chunks in four orders: left-to-right, right-to-left, middle-first, alternating
- After each pattern, requests a block larger than any single freed chunk to confirm merging worked

tests/test4.c (edge cases)
- malloc(10000) and malloc(4096) return NULL, non-multiples-of-8 sizes handled correctly, all pointers aligned across sizes 1-16, 20 repeated alloc/free cycles leave heap consistent

tests/test5.c (error: free on stack variable)
- Calls free(&x) where x is a local int, caught by bounds check

tests/test6.c (error: free on mid-chunk pointer)
- Calls free(p+1) which lands 4 bytes past the payload start, caught by heap walk

tests/test7.c (error: double free)
- Frees the same block twice via two pointers, caught by allocation flag check

tests/test8.c (leak detection)
- Allocates 100, 200, and 50 byte objects and never frees them, leak detector reports at exit

------------------------------------------------------------

Running the tests:

make              # compile everything
./memtest         # 0 incorrect bytes
./memgrind        # timing output for all 5 tasks
./test1           # Test 1 passed!
./test2           # Test 2 passed!
./test3           # Test 3 passed!
./test4           # Test 4 passed!
./test8           # check stderr for leak report
make test         # run all of the above together
make test-errors  # run error tests separately
./test5           # free() on stack variable
./test6           # free() on mid-chunk pointer
./test7           # double free

------------------------------------------------------------

Expected Results:
- memtest: "0 incorrect bytes"
- memgrind: timing lines for all 5 tasks, no stderr output
- test1-test4: "Test N passed!"
- test8: "mymalloc: NNN bytes leaked in 3 objects." on stderr
- test5, test6, test7: "free: Inappropriate pointer ..." on stderr, exit 2